'''Brian Thompson''' {{born|28|August|1959}} is an actor from Ellensburg, Washington who appeared in several episodes of {{s|TNG}}, {{s|DS9}} and {{s|ENT}}, as well as in {{film|7}}. His costume from the episode {{e|Rules of Acquisition}} was sold off on the [[It's A Wrap! sale and auction]] on eBay. {{stala|9683}}

He made his big screen debut in 1984 with a small role in {{wt|The Terminator}} (starring [[Linda Hamilton]], with [[Dick Miller]], [[Paul Winfield]], and [[Earl Boen]]). 

He is probably best known in his role as the alien bounty hunter on ''[[The X-Files]]''. His other TV credits include guest spots on {{wt|Falcon Crest}}, {{wt|Charmed}} and {{wt|Buffy the Vampire Slayer}}. He appeared as Eddie Fiore in {{wt|Kindred: The Embraced}}, starring [[Stacy Haiduk]], with [[Jeff Kober]], and as an eccentric Florida sheriff on {{wt|Key West (TV series)|Key West}}, that also starred [[Denise Crosby]], [[T.C. Carson]], [[Geno Silva]], [[Leland Crooke]] and [[Jennifer Barlow]]. He is also one of the many actors who have appeared in both the ''Star Trek'' and ''[[Babylon 5]]'' franchises, guest-starring in an episode of {{wt|Crusade (TV series)|Crusade}} with [[Gary Cole]], [[Tracy Scoggins]], [[Marjean Holden]] and [[Daniel Dae Kim]]. He has also had numerous roles in including {{wt|Cobra (1986 film)|Cobra}} (1986, with [[Andrew Robinson]], [[Roger Aaron Brown]], [[Laura Drake]], [[Leslie Morris]], and [[Kurt V. Hulett]]), {{wt|Three Amigos}} (1986, with [[Tony Plana]]), {{wt|Alien Nation (film)|Alien Nation}} (1988, with Earl Boen, [[Jeff Kober]], Roger Aaron Brown, [[Leslie Bevis]], [[Keone Young]], [[Tom Morga]], and [[Frank Collison]]), {{wt|Moon 44}} (1990, with [[Malcolm McDowell]] and [[Leon Rippy]]), {{wt|Ted & Venus}} (1991, with [[Lily Mariye]], [[Carol Kane]], [[Tricia O'Neil]], [[Rob Moran]] and [[Vincent Schiavelli]]), {{wt|Dragonheart}} (1996, with [[Dina Meyer]] and [[Jason Isaacs]]), {{wt|Doctor Mordrid}} (1992, starring [[Jeffrey Combs]]), {{wt|Mortal Kombat Annihilation}} (1997, with [[Musetta Vander]]), and {{wt|Joe Dirt}} (2001). 

