'''Gregory Itzin''' {{Born|20|April|1948|died|8|July|2022}} was an actor who appeared in five episodes spanning three spin-off series of the ''[[Star Trek]]'' franchise. He was first cast as either [[Whalen]] or [[McNary]] in the {{S|TNG}} [[TNG Season 1|first season]] episode {{E|The Big Goodbye}}, but dropped out in favor of doing an episode of ''L.A. Law'' instead, something he later expressed regret for.

Outside of ''Trek'', he may have been best known for his Emmy Award-nominated role as Vice President (and later President) Charles Logan on {{wt|24 (TV series)|24}}. His co-stars on this series included fellow ''Trek'' veterans [[Jude Ciccolella]], [[Glenn Morshower]], [[Roger Cross]], [[Peter Weller]], [[Ray Wise]], [[Tzi Ma]], [[Daniel Dae Kim]], [[Tony Todd]], [[Raphael Sbarge]], [[John Berg]] and [[Tom Wright]]. He once again popped up on the show for its sixth season in 2007, during which time [[James Cromwell]], [[Maury Sterling]] and Itzin's {{S|DS9}} co-star [[Alexander Siddig]] were also members of the cast.

He also earned a Tony Award nomination for his role in the 1993 Broadway play ''The Kentucky Cycle'', during which he acted alongside fellow ''Trek'' actors [[Scott MacDonald]] and [[Randy Oglesby]].

In 2010, he starred in the audio drama ''The Physicists'', produced by LA Theatreworks, co-starring [[Harry Groener]], [[John de Lancie]] and [[Bruce Davison]].

His friend and ''24'' producer Jon Cassar reported that Itzin had died on July 8, 2022. {{EL|radiotimes.com/tv/drama/gregory-itzin-dead-24-newsupdate/}} Itzin's manager later confirmed that he had passed away due to complications that arose from emergency surgery. {{EL|ew.com/celebrity/gregory-itzin-dead-24-actor-dies-at-74/}}

== Television ==
Itzin began appearing on television in the late 1970s. One of his first roles was a bit part in the 1979 mini-series ''Backstairs at the White House'', which also featured [[Ian Abercrombie]], {{Dis|John Anderson|actor}}, [[Bibi Besch]], [[Robert Hooks]], [[Bill Quinn]], [[Noble Willingham]] and [[Paul Winfield]]. He went on to become a familiar face in episodic television, guest starring on such shows as {{wt|Hunter (1984 American TV series)|Hunter}} (1985, with [[Nana Visitor]]), {{wt|Falcon Crest}} (with Bibi Besch, [[Robert Foxworth]] and [[Jonathan Frakes]]), {{wt|The A-Team}} (starring [[Dwight Schultz]]), {{wt|St. Elsewhere}}, {{wt|Matlock (1986 TV series)|Matlock}} (including a two-parter with [[Scott Bakula]]), {{wt|L.A. Law}} (working with [[James Avery]], [[Corbin Bernsen]], [[Bill Cobbs]], [[Larry Drake]] and [[Anne Haney]]), ''Night Court'' (starring [[John Larroquette]]), {{wt|Murphy Brown}}, {{wt|Beverly Hills, 90210}}, {{wt|Quantum Leap (1989 TV series)|Quantum Leap}} (1993, starring Scott Bakula and [[Dean Stockwell]], with [[Mary Elizabeth McGlynn]]), {{wt|Picket Fences}} (with [[Roy Brocksmith]], [[Ray Walston]] and Paul Winfield), and {{wt|Murder, She Wrote}} (1995, with [[Bill Smitrovich]]).

He was a regular on {{wt|Murder One (TV series)|Murder One}}, which also featured [[Daniel Benzali]], [[Barbara Bosson]], [[John Fleck]], [[Kevin Tighe]], [[Donna Murphy]], [[Conor O'Farrell]], [[Adam Scott]], [[Ned Vaughn]], [[Juliana Donald]] and [[Bruce Wright]] in regular and recurring roles. He was later seen on {{wt|Chicago Hope}} (1998, starring [[Roxanne Hart]] and [[Jayne Brook]], with [[Ethan Phillips]]), {{wt|Millennium (TV series)|Millennium}} (with [[Terry O'Quinn]] and Bill Smitrovich), {{wt|The Practice}} (with [[Bruce McGill]], [[Leon Russom]], [[George D. Wallace]] and [[Alfre Woodard]]), {{wt|JAG (TV series)|JAG}}, {{wt|Profiler (TV series)|Profiler}} (in a recurring role, with [[Matt McCoy]], [[Andrew Robinson]], [[Raphael Sbarge]] and [[Ray Wise]]), {{wt|CSI: Crime Scene Investigation}} (starring [[Paul Guilfoyle]], with [[Glenn Morshower]]), {{wt|Wolf Lake}} (starring [[Sharon Lawrence]] and [[Mia Kirshner]], with Bruce McGill and [[Kellie Waymire]]), {{wt|Firefly (TV series)|Firefly}} (alongside [[Ron Glass]] and [[Jordan Lund]]), {{wt|NYPD Blue}}, ''[[Friends]]'' (2002 & 2004, former with [[Cristine Rose]]), and {{wt|The Lyon's Den}} (with [[Jim Beaver]] and [[Robert Picardo]]). He also appeared opposite [[William Shatner]] and [[René Auberjonois]] in the two-part second season opener of {{wt|Boston Legal}}, along with [[Randy Oglesby]].

He also had a number of made-for-TV movies to his credit. In 1983 he was seen in {{wt|The Other Woman (1983 film)|The Other Woman}}, along with future {{S|VOY}} star Robert Picardo. In 1998 he appeared along with [[Wil Wheaton]], [[Donna Murphy]] and [[Titus Welliver]] in ''The Day Lincoln Was Shot''. And in 2003 he appeared in {{wt|DC 9/11: Time of Crisis}}, in which he played US Attorney General John Ashcroft. His co-stars included [[Penny Johnson]], [[Stephen Macht]], [[Lawrence Pressman]], [[Carolyn Scott]] and {{S|TOS}} star [[George Takei]].

Itzen appeared on [[CBS Broadcasting|CBS]]' {{wt|The Mentalist}} as California Bureau of Investigation director Virgil Minelli. He also reprised his role as Charles Logan in Season 8 of ''24''. He later recurred on {{wt|Big Love}} (2011), {{wt|Desperate Housewives}} (2011), and {{wt|Covert Affairs}} (2010-13).

== Film ==
Itzin was also no stranger to feature films. He made his debut in the medium with a role in {{wt|Airplane!}} (1980, featuring [[Jonathan Banks]], [[Kenneth Tobey]] and [[Jason Wingreen]]). This was followed in 1989 with a supporting role in ''The Fabulous Baker Boys'', co-starring [[Albert Hall]] and [[Dakin Matthews]].

One of his largest roles came in {{wt|Born to Be Wild (1995 film)|Born to Be Wild}} (1995, co-starring [[Alan Ruck]], [[Titus Welliver]] and {{S|ENT}} star [[John Billingsley]]. He later had supporting roles in {{wt|Fear and Loathing in Las Vegas (film)|Fear and Loathing in Las Vegas}} (1998, starring [[Benicio del Toro]], with [[Larry Cedar]], [[Jenette Goldstein]] and [[Richard Riehle]]), {{wt|Small Soldiers}} (1998, with [[Kirsten Dunst]], [[Dick Miller]], Robert Picardo, [[Ann Magnuson]] and [[Wendy Schaal]]), ''What's Cooking?'' (2000, with [[Alfre Woodard]]), {{wt|Evolution (2001 film)|Evolution}} (2001, with [[John Cho]], [[Miriam Flynn]], [[Michael McGrady]], [[Jennifer Savidge]] and [[Sarah Silverman]]), {{wt|Original Sin (2001 film)|Original Sin}} (2001, with [[Joan Pringle]]), and {{wt|Adaptation (film)|Adaptation}} (2002, with [[Jim Beaver]] and [[Doug Jones]]), among others.

Itzin appeared in a leading role in the drama ''Float'' (2008), again playing husband and wife with Cristine Rose. [[Kyla Kuhner]] served as co-producer for this film. Itzin later acted alongside [[Mark Harelik]], [[Jack Kehler]] and [[Ron Perlman]] in ''The Job'', a film which premiered at the San Diego Film Festival in September 2009. The same year he appeared in {{wt|Law Abiding Citizen}}, which also starred Bruce McGill and ''Next Generation''/''Deep Space Nine'' regular [[Colm Meaney]].''

He also appeared in {{wt|Lincoln (film)|Lincoln}} (2012), documenting the efforts of President [[Abraham Lincoln]] to abolish [[slavery]] during the [[American Civil War]], again appearing with Bruce McGill, as well as [[Grainger Hines]] and [[Dakin Matthews]].

Itzin was one of sixteen working character actors interviewed for {{wt|That Guy... Who Was in That Thing}} (2012). Several of the actors featured had made appearances in various ''Star Trek'' episodes (a fact the documentary notes), including [[Bruce Davison]], [[Zach Grenier]], [[Robert Joy]], [[Stanley Kamel]], [[Matt Malloy]], [[Mark Rolston]], [[W. Morgan Sheppard]], [[Wade Andrew Williams]] and [[Rick Worthy]].

